{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790284734,"nonce":"vkzqDhfpL49t8HnBhmi1fwHGmDk=","title":"Lua: a library function replaced by a field store is still called as the builtin","message":"builtin_member resolves `lib.name` statically whenever the global `lib` itself is never assigned, but a store into the library table does not count as an assignment:\n\n    math.sqrt = function(x) return x + 1 end\n    print(math.sqrt(4))\n\nlua5.4 prints 5, zylua prints 2.0 (at main 678f49eb). Any `lib.name = v`, `lib[k] = v`, `rawset(lib, ...)` or a library table escaping into code the types do not follow should make builtin_member refuse that library (or that member), the same way global_writes does for the global itself. Related to 9398fa2 (builtins resolve statically whatever _ENV holds), which is about the environment table, not member stores.","files":null}]}